      "title": "INTRODUCTION",
      "kind": "intro",
      "page": 0,
      "text_preview": "T HE IEEE 802.11b standard provides short-range data communications in a local area network (LAN) supporting data rates of up to 11 Mb/s. The data is modulated by BPSK, QPSK, or CCK, and further mapped by a direct-sequence spread-spectrum (DS-SS) technique [1]. On the other hand, Bluetooth, intended for wireless personal area network (WPAN) applications, supports a moderate data rate of 1 Mb/s. Th ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "II",
      "title": "THE DUAL-MODE RADIO ARCHITECTURE",
      "kind": "proposed",
      "page": 0,
      "text_preview": "The dual-mode radio integrates all the receive and transmit building blocks, such as voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO), frequency synthesizer, and channel-select ﬁlters (Fig. 1). On the receive path, a multiplexer separates the Bluetooth and 802.11b IF sections from the shared front-end. The multiplexer is controlled by the protocol software through baseband. The Bluetooth receiver beneﬁts from  ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "III",
      "title": "CIRCUIT IMPLEMENTATIONS",
      "kind": "design",
      "page": 2,
      "text_preview": "A. Receiver Front-End The receiver front-end consists of a 2.4-GHz LNA, followed Gilbert-type mixers which downcovert to either zero by or 2 MHz IF. On-chip active-RC ﬁlters provide channel selection. The active-RC topology is chosen due to its superior dynamic range to the other kinds of active ﬁlter implementation [6], [7]. The LNA is an inductively degenerated common-source ampliﬁer [8] whose g ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "IV",
      "title": "TRANSCEIVER MEASUREMENT RESULTS",
      "kind": "results",
      "page": 5,
      "text_preview": "The dual-mode transceiver IC is fabricated in TSMC 0.35m CMOS process. It integrates all the receive and transmit building blocks, such as LNA, VCO, and frequency synthesizer. Fig. 12 . shows the die photo, which occupies an area of about 25 mm About 60% of the die is common between the two standards. The IF section of each mode takes another 20%. All the pads, including the RF pads use full elect ..."
    },
    {
      "number": "V",
      "title": "CONCLUSION",
      "kind": "conclusion",
      "page": 7,
      "text_preview": "A highly integrated dual-mode transceiver in 0.35m CMOS intended for use in 802.11b and Bluetooth applications is reported. The radio is capable of providing a broadband internet access, as well as supporting several PAN applications. The low-IF Bluetooth receiver overcomes the ﬂicker noise and dc offset issues, whereas a zero-IF 802.11b receiver is essential for low power consumption and high int ..."
    }
